                <h2>为什么充值、消耗、发票必须放在一起对</h2>
<p>海外广告投放的账至少有三条线：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>付款线</strong>——以渔数媒账户充值、代理账户扣款、信用卡/PayPal/电汇付款；</li>
<li><strong>消耗线</strong>——Meta、Google、TikTok 后台的实际扣费、花费刷新与账单金额；</li>
<li><strong>票据线</strong>——代理商发票、平台账单、税务凭证（部分市场还需要 VAT/GST 抵扣）。</li>
</ol>
<p>三条线的时间口径完全不同：充值即时到账，消耗按日结（部分平台 T+1 刷新），发票可能滞后 30–60 天。于是运营常遇到：后台显示还有余额，钱却已经没了；或者账户已充值，发票迟迟没来。<strong>对账不是月底补账，而是日结 + 月结的固定动作。</strong></p>
<h2>第 1 步：建立三张基础表</h2>
<p>不要用同一张表记所有事。建议固定三张：</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>表格</th>
<th>关键字段</th>
<th>更新频率</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>充值流水表</td>
<td>日期、币种、充值金额、支付方式、付款凭证号、到账时间</td>
<td>每笔实时登记</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>消耗明细表</td>
<td>日期、平台、账户 ID、campaign、币种、消耗金额、后台账单编号</td>
<td>每日</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>发票台账</td>
<td>发票号、抬头、金额、税率、开票日期、对应充值区间、状态</td>
<td>收到即登记</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>三张表用<strong>账户 ID + 币种 + 日期区间</strong>互相勾稽，缺一张就无法闭环。</p>
<h2>第 2 步：充值当日做“到账确认”</h2>
<p>操作要点：</p>
<ul>
<li>记录付款时间、金额、币种、汇率折算口径（统一用记账汇率，避免多币种误差）；</li>
<li>确认到账后，在充值流水表回填“到账时间”，超出承诺时效（如电汇 3 个工作日）立即标记；</li>
<li>充值到平台广告账户时，备注“客户名 + 用途 + 订单号”，方便后续反查；</li>
<li>保留付款凭证截图并归档到统一目录，命名格式：<code>日期_客户_币种_金额_凭证</code>。</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>小技巧：多账户批量充值时，用一张“充值分配表”记录总充值金额如何分摊到各账户 ID 与各币种账户。月底差账 90% 都在这一层丢失信息。</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>第 3 步：每日导出消耗明细，而不是月底导</h2>
<p>每天固定时间（建议上午 10 点前）从各平台后台导出昨日消耗：Meta 走账单/Transactions，Google Ads 走 Billing &amp; Payments 账单明细，TikTok 走 Agency 广告账户账单。</p>
<p>导出后立刻做两件事：</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>与前一日余额变动做勾稽</strong>：昨日余额 − 昨日消耗 ± 退款/调整 = 今日余额，不等就标红；</li>
<li><strong>异常消耗预警</strong>：单日消耗突增 50% 以上、单账户余额 24 小时内掉到 0、单条 campaign 花费超预算 80%，全部当日上报，而不是等月底。</li>
</ol>
<h2>第 4 步：设置余额预警与自动停投线</h2>
<p>对账的价值一半在事前。建议按账户设置三档阈值：</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>余额 30%</strong>：通知运营 + 客户；</li>
<li><strong>余额 10%</strong>：提醒充值；</li>
<li><strong>余额 5% 或低于日均消耗 1 天</strong>：暂停新 campaign 计划或切到备用支付方式。</li>
</ul>
<p>余额预警要以“可用余额”而非“未消耗额度”为准，避免平台预扣、赠金、拒付（chargeback）造成的误判。</p>
<h2>第 5 步：月结时按“账户维度”出对账表</h2>
<p>每月结账日给客户发的对账单，建议一页纸呈现：</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>项目</th>
<th>金额</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>期初余额</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>本期充值</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>本期消耗</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>平台赠金/退款/调整</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>期末余额</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>后附明细消耗表（按账户 + 日期 + campaign）。<strong>期末余额必须用公式校验</strong>，不要手输。同时在表尾写明：账单周期、汇率口径、是否含税、差异说明。</p>
<h2>第 6 步：发票与充值/消耗的匹配逻辑</h2>
<p>发票通常滞后，先按以下规则预匹配：</p>
<ul>
<li>代理商发票 → 对应充值流水表里的订单号与付款区间；</li>
<li>平台直开账单（部分媒体提供 invoice）→ 对应消耗明细表的账单编号与账期；</li>
<li>一张开票多笔充值，用“开票金额 = 区间内各笔充值之和”校验；</li>
<li>拆分开票的，标注每张发票覆盖的消耗账期。</li>
</ul>
<p>登记时把发票状态分成四类：<strong>已开待付、已开已付、待开票、已开票信息有误</strong>。最后一类直接联系开票方更正，比事后做红冲快得多。</p>
<h2>第 7 步：差异处理与归档闭环</h2>
<p>常见差异与处理顺序：</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>现象</th>
<th>优先排查</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>余额与消耗对不上</td>
<td>汇率口径、赠金、预扣、跨日归因</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>消耗有、发票无</td>
<td>代理商开票周期、账户主体抬头不一致</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>充值有、消耗无</td>
<td>是否仍在赠金/预授权、是否被拒付撤销</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>发票金额偏高</td>
<td>是否含服务费、是否按含税价开</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>消耗异常突增</td>
<td>投放结构变更、授权额度被改、账户被盗用</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>处理完必须回写三张表的状态字段，并在对账群里留一条文字结论（谁处理、什么时候、结论是什么）。<strong>没有结论的差异不算关闭。</strong></p>
<h2>常见问答</h2>
<p><strong>Q：多币种账户怎么处理？</strong> 统一用记账汇率折算，汇率取付款当日中间价并在表头注明；涉及客户扣款时，保留原币金额。</p>
<p><strong>Q：赠金（credit）要不要算收入？</strong> 不算。对账单中单独列示，消耗优先冲抵赠金部分要写清楚，否则客户会认为平台“少扣了”。</p>
<p><strong>Q：谁负责这张流程？</strong> 建议投放运营负责日消耗与余额，财务负责充值、发票与月结，客服/AM 负责客户沟通与差异说明——三方各管一段，交汇在对账表。</p>
<h2>一句话原则</h2>
<p>充值有据、消耗有表、发票有主、差异有结论，这四件事做到，对账就不再是月底的救火，而是每周都能出结论的例行动作。</p>
            </div>
            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Why Payments, Spend, and Invoices Must Be Reconciled Together</h2>
<p>Overseas ad account finance runs on three separate tracks:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Payments</strong> — top-ups through Yishu Digital Media, agency account deductions, credit card, PayPal, or wire transfers;</li>
<li><strong>Spend</strong> — actual charges and refreshed spend figures across Meta, Google, and TikTok backends;</li>
<li><strong>Invoices</strong> — agency invoices, platform bills, and tax documentation (VAT/GST credits in some markets).</li>
</ol>
<p>Their timing differs: top-ups land instantly, spend settles daily (often T+1), invoices can lag 30–60 days. That is why ops teams see "balance left but money gone" or "money topped up, invoice never arrived." <strong>Reconciliation is not a month-end cleanup task — it is a daily and monthly routine.</strong></p>
<h2>Step 1: Build Three Core Sheets</h2>
<p>Keep them separate:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Sheet</th>
<th>Key fields</th>
<th>Update frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Top-up log</td>
<td>Date, currency, amount, payment method, transaction reference, credited date</td>
<td>Real-time per transaction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend detail</td>
<td>Date, platform, account ID, campaign, currency, spend amount, backend bill number</td>
<td>Daily</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice tracker</td>
<td>Invoice no., entity, amount, tax rate, issue date, covered top-up range, status</td>
<td>On receipt</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Tie the three together using <strong>account ID + currency + date range</strong>. If any one sheet is missing, the loop cannot close.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Confirm Crediting on the Day of Payment</h2>
<ul>
<li>Log payment time, amount, currency, and the exchange-rate convention (use one bookkeeping rate to avoid multi-currency drift);</li>
<li>Backfill the credited date once funds land; flag anything beyond the agreed SLA (e.g., 3 business days for wires);</li>
<li>When transferring funds to a platform ad account, tag it with "client name + purpose + order no." so it can be traced later;</li>
<li>Archive payment receipts in a single folder using <code>Date_Client_Currency_Amount_Receipt</code>.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p>Tip: For multi-account batch top-ups, keep an allocation sheet showing how the total is split across account IDs and currency accounts. Most month-end discrepancies originate from lost information at this layer.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Step 3: Export Spend Daily, Not at Month-End</h2>
<p>At a fixed time each morning (before 10:00 is common), export yesterday's spend from each platform: Meta via Bills/Transactions, Google Ads via Billing &amp; Payments, TikTok via agency ad account billing.</p>
<p>Then do two things immediately:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tie out against the balance change</strong>: yesterday's balance − yesterday's spend ± refunds/adjustments = today's balance. Flag anything that does not tie;</li>
<li><strong>Raise anomaly alerts</strong>: spend up 50%+ vs. the prior day, balance hitting zero within 24 hours, or a campaign at 80%+ of budget — escalate the same day, not at month-end.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Step 4: Set Balance Alerts and Auto-Pause Thresholds</h2>
<p>Half the value of reconciliation happens before the invoice. Use three tiers per account:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>30% remaining</strong>: notify ops and the client;</li>
<li><strong>10% remaining</strong>: prompt a top-up;</li>
<li><strong>5% remaining, or below one day of average spend</strong>: pause new campaign plans or switch to a backup payment method.</li>
</ul>
<p>Base alerts on <strong>available balance</strong>, not unused credit, so platform holds, grants, and chargebacks do not distort the signal.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Issue a Per-Account Monthly Reconciliation Statement</h2>
<p>The month-end statement sent to clients should fit on one page:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Line</th>
<th>Amount</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Opening balance</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Top-ups this period</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend this period</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Platform grants / refunds / adjustments</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Closing balance</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Attach the detailed spend table (account + date + campaign). <strong>Calculate the closing balance with formulas — never type it manually.</strong> State the billing period, exchange-rate convention, tax treatment, and any variance notes on the statement.</p>
<h2>Step 6: Match Invoices to Top-Ups and Spend</h2>
<p>Invoices usually arrive late, so pre-match using these rules:</p>
<ul>
<li>Agency invoices → the order number and payment window in the top-up log;</li>
<li>Platform-issued bills → the bill number and billing period in the spend detail sheet;</li>
<li>One invoice covering multiple top-ups → verify invoice amount equals the sum of those top-ups;</li>
<li>Split invoices → tag which spend period each invoice covers.</li>
</ul>
<p>Track four statuses: <strong>issued &amp; unpaid, issued &amp; paid, pending issuance, and issued with errors</strong>. The last category should be corrected with the issuer directly — far faster than issuing red-ink adjustments later.</p>
<h2>Step 7: Resolve Variances and Close the Loop</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Symptom</th>
<th>First checks</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Balance and spend don't tie</td>
<td>Exchange-rate convention, grants, holds, cross-day attribution</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend exists but no invoice</td>
<td>Agency invoicing cycle, mismatched legal entity name</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Top-up exists but no spend</td>
<td>Still covered by grants/pre-authorization, or reversed by a chargeback</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice amount too high</td>
<td>Service fee included, tax-inclusive pricing</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend spikes unexpectedly</td>
<td>Bidding or structure changes, spending limits altered, account compromise</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>After resolution, write the status back into all three sheets and post a written conclusion in the reconciliation channel (who handled it, when, and the outcome). <strong>A variance without a written conclusion is not closed.</strong></p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: How do we handle multi-currency accounts?</strong> Convert with one bookkeeping rate (mid-market on the payment date) and note it in the header; keep original currency amounts for client billing.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Do platform grants count as revenue?</strong> No. Show them as a separate line and clarify whether spend was offset against grants first, otherwise clients will think the platform "under-charged."</p>
<p><strong>Q: Who owns this workflow?</strong> Typically media buying ops handles daily spend and balances, finance handles top-ups, invoices and month-end close, and the AM/CS team handles client communication and variance explanations — three owners, one shared reconciliation sheet.</p>
<h2>The Core Principle</h2>
<p>Payments backed by evidence, spend backed by a sheet, invoices backed by an owner, variances backed by a conclusion. Do those four things and reconciliation stops being month-end firefighting and becomes a weekly, closeable routine.</p>
